T-D06 contract for a window containing zero closed and zero reopened items
of any type:

  exit code: 0 (an honest empty result is NOT a failure -- distinct from
  any usage-error or DB-unreadable exit path this tool may define)

  body (task-line's exact phrasing, T091): a message equivalent to
    "no data in window [from, to]"
  which -- by cross-tool convention with cycle_report.py's own CT-008
  ("NO_DATA_IN_WINDOW") -- is expressed structurally as:
    { "state": "NO_DATA_IN_WINDOW", "window": { "from": "2020-01-01", "to": "2020-01-02" } }

MUST NOT appear in the output: a "by_type" block populated with
zero-valued rate: 0.0 entries presented as real measurements (a rate of
0.0 for an empty window is not the same fact as "no data exists to measure
a rate from" -- CT-004/data-model.md V-CR-2's "no invention" rule, reused
here by the same cross-tool convention: a computed zero standing in for an
absent measurement is indistinguishable from a genuine zero-reopen result
unless the state is named explicitly); an "overall" block with a numeric
rate; a non-zero exit code.

This fixture's dates (2020-01-01..2020-01-02) are reused verbatim, byte-
for-byte, from the sibling test suite's
tests/fixtures/cycle_report/empty_window/window.json fixture -- the SAME
rationale (predates every commit and every tracked row) and the SAME
literal bounds, so both fastcycle tools' empty-window behaviour is tested
against one, single, already-verified-empty window rather than two
independently-invented ones (§11.4.227: reuse the settled pattern).

Paired-mutation target this fixture protects against (T093): reporting a
computed 0.0 rate (or an object with "reopened": 0, "closed": 0, "rate":
0.0) instead of the honest NO_DATA_IN_WINDOW state for every type and
overall.
